	abstract = {Artificial Intelligence (AI) is transforming modern aquaculture by integrating real-time sensing, predictive analytics, and autonomous decision-making into production systems. Advanced sensor networks, Internet of Things (IoT) platforms, and machine learning models enable continuous monitoring of water quality, fish behavior, and environmental fluctuations, addressing the limitations of traditional manual assessment. AI-driven optimization of feeding, aeration, and harvesting, supported by multivariate temporal data and significantly improves feed conversion ratios (FCR), reduces energy use, and minimizes operational waste. Innovations in computer vision further enhance precision through automated biomass estimation, stock assessment, and intelligent regulation of stocking density. Additionally, AI-enabled disease surveillance systems using behavioral tracking, anomaly detection, and thermal imaging allow early pathogen identification and reduce reliance on antibiotics. Emerging tools such as autonomous drones, robotic platforms, predictive biosecurity systems, and blockchain-based traceability strengthen operational transparency and regulatory compliance. This review synthesizes these advancements and presents an integrated AI framework designed to improve system resilience, sustainability, and production efficiency. The novelty lies in unifying real-time monitoring, predictive modeling, autonomous health management, and secure traceability into a cohesive digital aquaculture paradigm that supports global seafood demand while safeguarding environmental integrity. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.}
