	doi = {10.1016/j.aquaculture.2026.743833},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033264198?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Aquaculture has emerged as a particularly promising agribusiness activity, especially with regard to tilapia production, due to its adaptability and high nutritional value. The production of tilapia fingerlings and juveniles is imperative for the sustainability of the sector, directly impacting productivity and future food security. The accurate measurement of body weight in fry and juveniles is imperative for the effective management of fish farms. However, conventional manual weighing methods can be invasive, induce stress in the fish, and result in operational inaccuracies. The utilisation of digital images provides a non-invasive, more accurate alternative and can also be automated, thereby expediting the process, minimising annotation errors and optimizing measurements. The objective of this study was to evaluate the efficacy of machine learning techniques in predicting the weight of juvenile tilapia (Oreochromis niloticus) using digital images. The experiment was conducted with juvenile tilapia, with weights ranging from 10 to 100 g. A total of 2092 images were collected using an iPhone 12 Pro Max under controlled conditions, ensuring the consistency and high quality of the photographs. The data underwent meticulous pre-processing and labelling on the Roboflow platform, with data augmentation techniques being employed to enhance the generalisability of the models and augment the robustness of the predictions. A range of machine learning algorithms were evaluated, encompassing SVM (Support Vector Machine), Decision Tree, KNN (K-Nearest Neighbors), XGBoost Regressor, Random Forest and Linear Regression. The data set was segmented into three equal parts: 70% was allocated for training, 20% for validation, and 10% for testing. The evaluation of all models revealed a high correlation between the weights estimated by the algorithms and the observed weights, as well as high coefficients of determination. This finding demonstrates the models' efficacy in predicting the weight of tilapia fry. However, the Linear Regression and Random Forest models were distinguished by their superior performance in terms of minimal mean square errors, thereby evidencing enhanced efficiency. The efficacy, reliability and practical application of these two models in the non-invasive monitoring and management of tilapia fry weight in aquaculture was demonstrated. The findings demonstrate that the integration of high-quality data, collected in a standardized manner, with robust statistical methodologies, can substantially enhance the precision of biomass estimates within aquaculture systems. The study corroborates the considerable potential of machine learning techniques, particularly Linear Regression and Random Forest, as efficacious tools for optimizing and modernising weighing management systems in aquaculture, exerting a substantial impact on the sustainability and efficiency of the sector. © 2026}
